#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807} :activity/id #uuid "eb431efc-453b-5866-adc7-559d3c697e3e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/id #uuid "40868547-c38a-58e8-b266-21013ff254f9" :topic/math-academy-id 3627} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519842} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519833}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "efebb741-9ea8-56f3-9f79-7c340d1fe301" :step/math-academy-id 33843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562815} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562808}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "736cedc4-b5c1-5e97-b975-c6db4fd154c1" :step/math-academy-id 35775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177037 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b5a23c7-4ccf-5122-8b5f-98fbabb0e367" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5838 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b3de726-ead0-5369-a1fe-3dcc61b38bbc" :step/math-academy-id 35776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563859} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563860}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "f69ccccf-5d34-5f08-95d0-7af5d0c7628b" :step/math-academy-id 35777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176145}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459335} :activity/id #uuid "9407eb8b-2b41-54d6-91e3-4f75b70fbcd7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/id #uuid "40868547-c38a-58e8-b266-21013ff254f9" :topic/math-academy-id 3627} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e66697d-f4a8-5190-9c92-04e431212fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458684} {:answer/id #uuid "21c3618c-e307-593c-be1d-ae62e518df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458696} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8d4fbc-5dde-5631-9343-463c2c790561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458790} {:answer/id #uuid "7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458831} {:answer/id #uuid "876615df-473a-5e4a-9661-1a6b746a5404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3be5c97-a9dc-5eec-ad89-fde98181ed37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" :question/math-academy-id "q-126677" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb2da6fbb22b19d027212685ca521f5186a831502617c2089766c5e9fe739696.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/48/4822bfa926feccb0a12c14580ac325f4f10a0e4f815d0247b2231bf08b8c2326.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33aa2c01-ef8c-530d-9a87-558d09200d7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458839} {:answer/id #uuid "8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458874} {:answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} {:answer/id #uuid "a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458925} {:answer/id #uuid "e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126735" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f3/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b39e3053-19ff-54d9-8a03-cabe47ece267" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458702} {:answer/id #uuid "64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458803} {:answer/id #uuid "8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458877} {:answer/id #uuid "b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458946} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126737" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb7edd78-bd6a-5d85-baab-eeb17426674d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458686} {:answer/id #uuid "648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458801} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} {:answer/id #uuid "76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458837} {:answer/id #uuid "867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/math-academy-id "q-126532" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d286d5c7-a12c-594f-9ccb-48543ab9d3ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14eb9fcd-7abf-50ea-acb9-8e896cb286b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458663} {:answer/id #uuid "4752d255-6d05-5b8c-9bc7-d3d564d74fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458759} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458767} {:answer/id #uuid "62d8034f-3c24-592c-b26b-73464d3851ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}({r}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458798} {:answer/id #uuid "778250fc-a408-537b-8277-d49912ff9021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458767} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a80e3-eaa7-5f33-b4a3-69d8d31c5ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-126707" :question/problem "![](images/18/184b50ce54b01453d5b645eef7edc186823e8ae34afaefa2527e34122c1722cc.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/93/93a1c20377ddd0731c6190712d9d6d77551124fa33001334e84f52049bb6b800.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1+2{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 9,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d88b6d01-4f98-5073-ad53-8b68c9498d2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459332}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458610}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564587} :activity/id #uuid "29f8ac3d-ebae-5ec0-91bf-2c2d11fca1b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/id #uuid "40868547-c38a-58e8-b266-21013ff254f9" :topic/math-academy-id 3627} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dd58ef-4b04-5352-bcdf-e5156b92820d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259917} {:answer/id #uuid "3509406d-87dd-5cbf-9357-f595d8330751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260348} {:answer/id #uuid "78fa28b7-4b07-5bd5-ac2c-f175adfb8d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260937} {:answer/id #uuid "ab43a82f-c1c3-5558-b7ba-045a5ed382c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261375} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ebf1d6-bf91-58cd-afe8-ad00f1045eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261375} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e1a501-c1de-55ec-8316-3866bf836e2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bfc83011-612b-5b59-8833-f11d956fe6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126710" :question/problem "![](images/83/831b2131cf134ec21def0014a3121429ded4cd3afd98dfd1660b1e78c1a9ba93.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y(x+1),\\,x(y+1)⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8cd55f88d614bdb49aee6eaa0ff9d1d56f6f597cd892ee199a0130b53954b5b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1-(x+1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b2453-2340-5b1a-b035-13af6216e390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260350} {:answer/id #uuid "678d8b1a-b4fb-5ecd-9b33-15222113a168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260779} {:answer/id #uuid "a144f162-db84-521f-aac9-d31fa7f5c9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261277} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d7ee8d-a24f-5376-9f95-9be5fc03201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261467} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e469d8-39d1-5c40-aeb7-1753437104ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d81e6d-01cf-501c-b61e-4c6039a60547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "388e817a-4cb8-54d8-b3b7-fede3e086b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126736" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\text{ }\\,\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/90/902790a00a049bbfc759403125ad5f19a8c2528a46b59c721ac43fb137cd5d8e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2081022a-bc13-504d-84ff-392ffcb85254" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e4cdf-338e-59c7-bd21-7afe84d888b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} {:answer/id #uuid "36abd901-c8ba-5d38-a5c9-52c30378dadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260363} {:answer/id #uuid "9d666cad-5bfd-565d-8739-349a89a1addf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{90\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bca1ec-b5b5-53a5-8432-35d6656bef12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261314} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5fdf8c-c1cf-53bf-9f78-dc3ffff862be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcd46d2-644b-54bb-992b-feba4db98108" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5041cff1-c6ce-564b-b2c4-39a44d615f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-126758" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcdc0f59fa284c2b1f1621e7de057ef6f0e084d2eca6732fbc5eae3f74ede113.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/62/6273034b38059d3d49b2a29c91052c290151b4a29073f1c925172dd1c4c2bf72.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{2}-(-3{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}60\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =180[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =180\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =90\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c0549f0-8417-57bd-b711-92eea5341e9e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf2dceb-7c5d-5d9b-9403-414a88730866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416074} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5d7b5b-5c88-5245-85a2-eb913d758a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{729\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416083} {:answer/id #uuid "96c867f3-7baa-5a33-8eba-e465e7463140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416090} {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} {:answer/id #uuid "abee63b5-df6a-5f86-838c-404f4f8edb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} :answer-field/id #uuid "07315ac9-2ead-596d-85c1-f7d066447650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "654eb25e-4dd2-5095-910d-a25787a1e455" :question/math-academy-id "q-126753" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af0a8f06-6ad0-508f-9f03-0538665d492d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} {:answer/id #uuid "45df6382-7cbc-520f-a94b-e0654045f8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257393} {:answer/id #uuid "80f5da35-1f8d-5b8e-98bc-a87edfbce6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257928} {:answer/id #uuid "d9166bd6-4167-5918-9672-1af0c9421726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258683} {:answer/id #uuid "e9823d2d-a2cd-57c0-bf4a-ee850ecc8da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca565c59-abfb-57f2-916c-26752c3de0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20585d4c-063f-50c6-8591-a785c62137ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-125719" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d38c598c-e6c8-596d-b5b1-344239cf7390" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564541}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563959}]}
